he flux of humours in them, as also the abundance of women’s courses, or any other fluxes of blood, being boiled in red wine, and drank. The yellow Rattle, or Cock’s Comb, is held to be good for those that are troubled with a cough, or dimness of sight, if the herb, being boiled with beans, and some honey put thereto, be drank or dropped into the eyes. The whole seed being put into the eyes, draws forth any skin, dimness or film, from the sight, without trouble, or pain.","spans":[{"start":104,"end":112,"concept_id":"skin_ulcers","polarity":"therapeutic"},{"start":124,"end":130,"concept_id":"skin_ulcers","polarity":"therapeutic"},{"start":185,"end":213,"concept_id":"menorrhagia","polarity":"therapeutic"},{"start":228,"end":243,"concept_id":"hemorrhage","polarity":"therapeutic"},{"start":371,"end":376,"concept_id":"cough","polarity":"therapeutic"},{"start":381,"end":397,"concept_id":"eye_disease","polarity":"therapeutic"},{"start":573,"end":577,"concept_id":"eye_disease","polarity":"therapeutic"}]}},"gloss":{"cul:250-rattle-grass":{"104":"fistula, fistulous ulcers","124":"ulcers (old, foul, running, fretting)","185":"immoderate / overflowing flux of the courses","228":"fluxes of blood","371":"cough, coughs","381":"dimness of sight","573":"film over the eye"}},"images":[]}